{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>To His face.</b> To the face of each one of them. <b>To destroy him,</b> as He destroyed the Egyptians, and they have no other. And this attribute that He mentioned is true forever: even though there is a wicked person whose evil is prolonged, it shall not be so for him except because of this attribute — that He preserves lovingkindness, and if [the wicked person] did some kindness that must be repaid to him. Thus wrote the Ramban, of blessed memory." "<b>And there are those who</b> explained אל פניו as 'for Himself,' in the manner of (II Samuel 17:11): ״ופניך הולכים בקרב״." "<b>He does not delay for His enemy.</b> That is, one should not think that He waits for him for his generations, thirty or forty, but rather to himself He repays him — this is the meaning of אל פניו ישלם לו, 'to his face He repays him.'" "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> 'and repays His enemies to their face' — this is what the verse says (Psalms 73:4): ״כי אין חרצובות במותם ובריא אולם״. What is חרצובות? The Holy One, blessed be He, does not delay the desire of the wicked in this world; rather, whatever they seek He gives them — like a sick person into whom the doctor comes to visit him: he took his arm and said, 'Give him whatever he wants to eat.' Why? Because he is dying. Thus Moses said to them: 'and repays His enemies to their face, to destroy him.' So they expounded in Midrash Tehillim. And they further expounded in another place: Rabbi Yeshayah said: For three things the Holy One, blessed be He, extends His anger toward the wicked in this world: perhaps they will repent; or they have already performed a commandment for which the Holy One, blessed be He, repays them reward in this world; or perhaps righteous people will emerge from them. For thus we find: He extended His anger to Ahaz, and Hezekiah emerged from him; He extended His anger to Amon, and Josiah emerged from him; to Shimei, and Mordecai emerged from him. Even though these are not included among 'His enemies,' for His enemies are the people who deny HaShem and say 'He is not,' and concerning them the verse says 'to destroy him.'" "<b>And the view</b> of Onkelos, of blessed memory, in this verse is that the Holy One, blessed be He, repays good reward to the wicked for the minor merits in his hand, in order to exact payment from him for the majority of his sins in the World to Come. And the Holy One, blessed be He, wishes to requite him with the inferior thing, which is the body, and in the lesser time, which is this world, in order to destroy him from the World to Come and to punish him with the noble thing, which is the soul, and in the superior time, which is the World to Come. If so, all his good in this world is only for his harm. And thus David, of blessed memory, explained (Psalms 92:7): ״איש בער לא ידע וכסיל לא יבין את זאת״ — that is, he does not understand the statutes of the attribute of justice, which is called זאת, how it exacts payment from the wicked. And this is what it says (ibid. 92:8): ״בפרוח רשעים כמו עשב ויציצו כל פועלי און להשמדם עדי עד״ — for their success and tranquility in this world is only for a moment, and therefore he compared them to the sprouting of grass, but their end is that they will perish from the World to Come. This is why he gave the reason: להשמדם עדי עד. And this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded (Proverbs 18:5): ״שאת פני רשע לא טוב״ — the countenance of the wicked in this world that is 'not good' is [in fact] good in the World to Come. And so the righteous person is the reverse of this: He punishes him for the minor sins in his hand, in order that he may be cleared and become worthy of the life of the World to Come."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:31.989425363Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}